></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>I mentioned last week tha=
t I have been working on what has variously been called =E2=80=9Cagentic co=
ding=E2=80=9D, =E2=80=9CAI coding=E2=80=9D and now =E2=80=9Charness enginee=
ring=E2=80=9D. Someone mentioned that perhaps not everyone understood what =
a harness is, or why they matter, and suggested I write about it this week.=
 Why not! </p><p><span>When GPT-4 was first shown to me at Microsoft almost=
 4 years ago, one of the first things I thought was =E2=80=9Cwell, there ar=
e a lot of people working on building models, I probably can=E2=80=99t help=
 with that, but I do know how to build software, so I=E2=80=99ll focus on b=
uilding WITH models instead=E2=80=9D. The first project my team and I worke=
d on in this direction was what was then called an =E2=80=9Corchestrator=E2=
=80=9D - ours was (and still is) called Semantic Kernel.</span><span style=
=3D"min-width: 0px;" data-state=3D"closed"><a data-component-name=3D"Footno=
teAnchorToDOM" id=3D"footnote-anchor-1" href=3D"https://sundaylettersfromsa=
m.substack.com/p/what-is-a-harness-and-why-do-i-care#footnote-1" target=3D"=
_self" class=3D"footnote-anchor">1</a></span></p><p>The kernel was very sim=
ple - just some bits of code that could =E2=80=9Corchestrate=E2=80=9D calls=
 to an AI model. How does that work? Well - most people encounter AI throug=
h the chat interface - you talk to it, wait for a response, etc. There=E2=
=80=99s another interface, an API, that you can call directly. It=E2=80=99s=
 similar - you send in a prompt as text - but you can do a bit more with it=
. You can control things like the =E2=80=9Ctemperature=E2=80=9D (how =E2=80=
=9Clow probability=E2=80=9D the responses are), stream token-by-token (inst=
ead of waiting for either upload or download) and so on. So with an orchest=
rator, you can do simple automation, like running a loop that calls the API=
 multiple times. You can take the response of one request and use it in cod=
e to make decisions about what the next request might be. One early thing w=
e did was called =E2=80=9Ccontext query=E2=80=9D, where the first request w=
as a set of questions and an instruction to return them in XML format, with=
 each categorized (=E2=80=9Cfact=E2=80=9D, =E2=80=9Cunknown=E2=80=9D, =E2=
=80=9Cneeds web lookup=E2=80=9D etc) and then a second pass would either ge=
nerate the answer directly, if known, or invoke a tool like search if neede=
d. It cut down on hallucinations. </p><p>Tool use like search is another th=
ing that harnesses can do. Modern harnesses allow models to make very sophi=
sticated use of tools. The one I=E2=80=99m working on lets a model, or agen=
t, create a =E2=80=9Csubagent=E2=80=9D and hand tasks off to it, as though =
it were a separate worker. Or it can create a whole =E2=80=9Ccontainer=E2=
=80=9D that isolates work into a new virtual environment. </p><p>Harnesses =
allow models to select tools and agents based on the task at hand, which gi=
ves much better results than =E2=80=9Cjust asking=E2=80=9D. We use a techni=
que called =E2=80=9Crecipes=E2=80=9D that are workflows based on a format c=
alled yaml - these orchestrate calls to the model into long, complex tasks.=
 This is what I used to build the Word clone I mentioned. This past week I =
built a large recipe, that I call a =E2=80=9Cfoundry=E2=80=9D that is capab=
le of writing books in my voice. It uses a huge collection of tools - custo=
m models to judge the writing, linguistic analysis tools, and some custom e=
diting workflows that interact with me. </p><p>Harnesses can also get aroun=
d, at least partially, one of the limits of AI models: memory. Even though =
an AI model seems like a person sometimes, it doesn=E2=80=99t really have t=
he same kind of mind as a person does - in particular, it can=E2=80=99t for=
m new memories. All new information has to be passed in, each time. One of =
the main uses of an agentic harness is to manage and orchestrate an externa=
l memory store. Keeping the =E2=80=9Ccontext=E2=80=9D that is passed to the=
 model in good shape is a hard problem with a number of different approache=
s that agentic harnesses can implement. </p><p>One of the challenges of wor=
king on a harness is that the models will often overtake some of the capabi=
lities in the harness - this has been true of memory, some tool use, and ot=
her kinds of reasoning and metacognition. So it makes harness development s=
omewhat of a moving target. My team has found that we have always been able=
 to get more out of a model with a harness, than without one, though - ther=
e=E2=80=99s never been a time when we couldn=E2=80=99t develop some new lev=
erage. The problems we can reach for just keep getting bigger. </p><p>And, =
even more interestingly, now that the models themselves understand code wel=
l, they can help with their own harness development. The harness we work on=
 (Microsoft Amplifier, open source on GitHub) has two different experts (or=
 tools, depending on how you look at them) that help. One is called =E2=80=
=9Csession analyst=E2=80=9D, which is a tool it can use to look at other ha=
rness sessions and understand how they work (this is actually a fairly hard=
 thing to do well - the amount of text in a large session can easily be mor=
e than the model itself can handle in one go, so it can=E2=80=99t just read=
 it all in - even just reading enough to understand what to read can overwh=
elm. It=E2=80=99s a little like reading out of the corner of your eye - has=
 to be done carefully). The other is a =E2=80=9Cfoundation expert=E2=80=9D =
that understands how the system itself is put together (a third, =E2=80=9CC=
rusty Old Engineer=E2=80=9D is my absolute favorite - a cynical engineer ag=
ent that gives really good design advice). Combined, these can often look a=
t a new capability, another codebase, or even a research paper, and work to=
gether to add new capabilities to the harness itself. Others have called th=
is =E2=80=9Cgene transfer=E2=80=9D and it works surprisingly well. </p><p><=
span>And of course, all of this compounds - as the harness builds tools, it=
 gets better at building the next ones. And the models get smarter, so they=
 get better at writing code and using the tools, and the harness can take a=
dvantage of that. Over time, we=E2=80=99ve gone from very simple tools like=
 Context Query, to the current ones that are building programs, books</span=
><span style=3D"min-width: 0px;" data-state=3D"closed"><a data-component-na=
me=3D"FootnoteAnchorToDOM" id=3D"footnote-anchor-2" href=3D"https://sundayl=
ettersfromsam.substack.com/p/what-is-a-harness-and-why-do-i-care#footnote-2=
" target=3D"_self" class=3D"footnote-anchor">2</a></span><span>, and more c=
omplex systems and artifacts over days or even weeks, with little or no sup=
ervision. </span></p><p>So=E2=80=A6that=E2=80=99s what a harness is. When y=
ou hear =E2=80=9CAI=E2=80=9D, it=E2=80=99s important to think about the fac=
t that it=E2=80=99s not one single thing. There are models, that do the cor=
e work of thinking, but then there are tools, data connectors, memory syste=
ms, and finally harnesses that orchestrate all of it into even more effecti=
ve systems. Each piece contributes something to the overall picture. </p><p=
